+## Resolution Scoring Labels (Source Contracts)
+
+`ResolutionScoringLabel` is an optional init property on `CreateConversationCommand`, `UpdateConversationMetadataCommand`, `ConversationCreated`, and `ConversationMetadataUpdated`. Its value is a `ConversationResolutionScoringLabel` with the exact `Value`, `Purpose = ConversationResolutionScoringLabel.SupportedPurpose` (`projects-internal-equality-scoring`), and `Classification = ConversationResolutionScoringLabel.OwnerClassification` (`conversations-owner-safe-metadata-equality-v1`). Opt-in is explicit: an ordinary `Label` does not acquire classification from its text. The creation aggregate copies accepted classification into the durable creation event; native event replay carries it into state and isolated resolution metadata.
+
+The value must equal the corresponding `Label`, preserve its original spelling and whitespace, and fit within 160 characters. Existing safe-metadata validation rejects blank text, control characters, paths, URLs, secrets and other reserved disclosure terms. Invalid purpose, classification, content or bounds are rejected without trimming or truncation. A classified `Value = null` explicitly records absence; it is valid only when the native label is also null. An omitted classification remains omitted in legacy JSON, and the existing positional constructors retain their signatures.
+
+A metadata event with a non-null `Label` and no classification invalidates prior scoring classification. Updates that change only attributes or the business reference preserve it. An explicitly classified native metadata event may reverify the supplied label; it contributes a later source event rather than rewriting prior history. Classification and purpose participate in command idempotency, so adding, changing or removing opt-in changes command meaning. Replayed duplicate creation and conflicting classification are rejected; observed invalid input blocks disclosure while preserving the prior completed generation.
+
+Older isolated metadata may contain an implicit classification but no persisted purpose. It remains unavailable for scoring. Redelivery of that original dispatch, including rebuild preparation, returns an unsupported-capability refusal before recovery, ledger or proof writes; its stored bytes are retained. Recovery requires a later supported native generation with explicit classification. Query execution does not migrate, repair or classify historical data.
+
+These are source contracts and replay behavior. This change adds no deployed metadata-mutation route or production writer enrollment. Mutation dispatch/adoption, current native owner authorization, Conversation guard enrollment, rebuild completion, installation coverage and qualification remain separate gates. The internal resolution metadata read additionally requires its existing verified delegation and current resource/Tenant proofs. Scoring classification grants no inspection or resource permission, and Projects RTI and parent resolution implementation remain gated.
+
 ## Typed Errors
 
 `ConversationError` is the canonical adopter-safe error contract. It exposes structured fields only: `code`, `category`, `isRetryable`, `clientAction`, `safeMessage`, `correlationId`, optional allowed `auditHandle`, `safeFieldDiagnostics`, and an HTTPS `documentation` pointer. `developerGuidance` remains for backward-compatible safe text, but adopter applications should branch on `code`, `category`, and `clientAction`.
